// Gen9 rise (op 11): a glass window's ground made from its small tinted picture, as the processor's compositor_rise and the grain of
// memory_glass_over_u32 make it (src/canvas/compositor/glass.c, src/lib.c): the small picture doubled three times, each pixel of a level
// three quarters of the one over it and a quarter of its neighbour across and down by byte means (memory_double_u32: v = (c + ((c + n + 1) >> 1)) >> 1,
// down the rows and then across), and the ordered grain of 0 to 3 levels added (dark glass, v = 0) or taken away (light, v != 0), clamped,
// the alpha byte left as it is. A block of the output is 8 by 8 pixels fixed to the output's grid, made from the three by three small
// pixels round its own. A thread is six blocks of a row (the card's time is the threads' more than their instructions'): walker group X
// (r0.1) their column of six and Y (r0.6) their row. It reads the three small rows once (binding table entry 1: 32 bytes, eight small
// pixels, by 3 rows at byte 24 X of row Y, so the source's first pixel is the block before the first across and above it) and writes each
// block (entry 0: 32 bytes by 8 rows at byte 192 X + 32 k of row 8 Y), as many as the row has left of w (CURBE). For a block the three
// small rows become four of level 2 (four pixels: the two under the block and one each side), those six of level 1 and those the eight of
// the block, a row a register, its B and R bytes in the words of one register and its G and A bytes in another, so a byte mean is a
// word's (avg, add and shr on sixteen words: no byte is a source, which Gen11 and later refuse); the small rows then move a pixel along.
// The grain's level at x, y is 2 * ((x ^ y) & 1) + (y & 1); a block starts at even x and y, so its even rows have 0 2 0 2 and its odd rows
// 3 1 3 1 across. CURBE: r1.0 = v, r1.1 = w (the blocks of a row). Written by the scratch generator genglass.py, which holds a block's
// steps to a model of the library's three doublings. Assemble with: iga64 -a -p=9 src/canvas/gpu_i915_rise.iga (the words are in
// src/canvas/gpu_i915.asm).
